7] [17592186162228 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "9c611ad4-705d-5751-a473-84f3e834b6d3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162345 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186388493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162345 #edb/ref 1069 "q-136973" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162345 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162345 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\nU=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}],\\,Σ=[\\begin{aligned}5\\sqrt{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,V=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{3}{5} & \\frac{4}{5} \\\\ \\frac{4}{5} & \\frac{3}{5}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above. Given that $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ find the pseudoinverse ${A}^{+}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162345 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "44a4660e-d3b5-5880-9cf8-ce9a4c5ec070" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162345 #edb/ref 1074 "If $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ then the pseudoinverse of $A$ is given by\n\n$$\nV\\,{Σ}^{+}\\,{U}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere ${Σ}^{+}$ is the pseudoinverse of $Σ.$\n\nFirst, notice that\n\n$$\nV=\\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 3\\end{aligned}],\\,{Σ}^{+}=\\frac{1}{5\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{U}^{T}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{+} & =V\\,{Σ}^{+}\\,{U}^{T} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 3\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{5\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{50}[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 3\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{50}[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{50}[\\begin{aligned}3 & -3 \\\\ -4 & 4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163031 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186163047 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163031 #edb/ref 1061 36962 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163031 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "a081b9a8-d058-5b79-8d8b-a8471a1f524a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163031 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186163306 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163032 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186163034 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163032 #edb/ref 1061 36963 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163032 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "b8379f09-2e11-58eb-bae4-01a9b4bd2b5b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163032 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186162089 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163033 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186163046 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163033 #edb/ref 1061 36966 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163033 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "a1fe2653-29e8-5527-b4fa-083f233b81c0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163033 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186162090 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163034 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186163035 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163034 #edb/ref 1061 36969 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163034 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "0c540186-c349-53ef-8094-366eb44f526a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163034 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186163307 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163035 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186163039 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163035 #edb/ref 1061 36970 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163035 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "03964208-b7f5-5c5d-ab41-385b66f7efbd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163035 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186162091 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163039 #edb/ref 1061 36979 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163039 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "f03c737f-a30a-5ef1-8ade-d5c1bd49e933" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163039 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186163309 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163046 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186163032 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163046 #edb/ref 1061 37091 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163046 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "cef3aa4e-1b1d-50ea-b285-5cc2dcac6f73" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163046 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186163313 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163047 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186163033 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163047 #edb/ref 1061 37092 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163047 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "e5289df4-d145-54b7-a6f3-e8e13a522ee7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163047 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186163314 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163306 #edb/ref 1064 "The invertible matrix theorem tells us that if $A$ is an $n \\times n$ matrix with full rank (i.e., $\\mathrm{rank}(A)=n$), then there exists a unique inverse matrix $A^{-1}$ such that \n\n$$\nAA^{-1}=A^{-1}\\!A=I_n,\n$$\n\nwhere $I_n$ denotes the $n \\times n$ identity matrix.\n\nThere are two questions that we can ask:\n\n- Can we extend the notion of an inverse matrix to one that works for $n\\times n$ matrices $A$ where $\\mathrm{rank}(A) < n?$\n\n- Is it possible to generalize the concept of an inverse to non-square matrices?\n\nThe answer to both questions is yes! This generalization of the inverse of $A$ is called the **pseudoinverse** of $A,$ and is denoted $A^+.$ The dimensions of $A^+$ are the same as $A^T.$\n\nAs an example, let's consider the ${\\color{red}{3}}\\times {\\color{blue}{2}}$ matrix $A$ and its corresponding pseudoinverse, given below:\n\n![](images/87/87581a69d3d76dbf3b0ebca8941c9a82b5b9443b00e86f32e4e9e4a42ef5e18b.svg)\n\n- Computing $A^{+}\\!A,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{+}\\,A & =\\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is the identity matrix $I_{\\color{blue}{2}}.$\n\n- Computing $A\\!A^{+},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A{A}^{+} & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is *almost* the identity matrix $I_{\\color{red}{3}}.$\n\nWe can explain this result using the following properties of the pseudoinverse:\n\n- *If the columns of an $m\\times {\\color{blue}{n}}$ matrix $A$ are linearly independent, then $A^+A = I_{\\color{blue}{n}}.$*\n\n- *If the rows of an ${\\color{red}{m}}\\times n$ matrix $A$ are linearly independent, then $AA^+ = I_{\\color{red}{m}}.$*\n\nIn the example above, the columns of $A$ are linearly independent, but the rows are not.\n\nMoreover, the notion of a pseudoinverse is consistent with inverse matrices. In particular,\n\n*If $A$ is an invertible square matrix, then $A^{+} = A^{-1}.$*\n\nThis lesson will discuss how to compute the pseudoinverse for any matrix, starting with an important special case." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163306 #edb/ref 1065 6074 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163306 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "6cec9ecf-529c-566f-9895-3fde09f24253" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163306 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163307 #edb/ref 1064 "Consider the matrix\n\n![](images/98/987aa533ca51a9f101ba590b7ade0753983ae219534342aad42054316b2e02ec.svg)\n\nand its singular value decomposition $A=U \\Sigma V ^T,$ where\n\n![](images/69/69e68ac94c6e25b2609f7d20616d7d564a1225abad748c3e4fd94516cb6df72f.svg)\n\nNotice that $\\Sigma$ contains a row of zeros. In these situations, it's possible to simplify the decomposition of $A.$ This simplified decomposition is called the **reduced singular value decomposition** of $A.$\n\nFirst, we note that $A$ is a $3 \\times 2$ matrix. In addition, the matrix $\\Sigma$ has two non-zero singular values on its main diagonal, so the rank of $A$ is $\\color{blue}r=2.$ \n\nTo find our reduced SVD, we proceed as follows:\n\n- First, we remove all zero rows and columns from $\\Sigma.$ In our example, we should delete the $3$rd row since it contains only zeros. Notice that we obtain a square invertible matrix of rank ${\\color{blue}2}{:}$ \n![](images/a5/a56440b8808003f25bec4b61d4d06af7274a5eea68da4e94af9e91af8d64d02a.svg)\n\n- Next, we remove all extra columns of $U$ starting from the right, keeping only $\\color{blue}2$ columns. In our example, we should delete the $3$rd column:\n![](images/e4/e4ba93ef3588affc421af821cb9de1051749396c69a9e64523399f7fc4e3bc5b.svg)\n\n- Finally, we remove all extra columns of $V$ starting from the right, keeping only $\\color{blue}2$ columns. In our example, we should keep $V$ as it is since it already has only $2$ columns: \n![](images/b1/b146b37075dda593ded5b86d58e6cfba3de463e01727b39756a6b90cbb88a95f.svg)\n\nThe reduced singular value decomposition of $A$ is then given by\n\n$$\nA = U_r \\Sigma_r V_r^T.\n$$\n\nSo, in our example, we have\n\n![](images/c1/c1eed35f9b7aa3214e9783f8f80952e57adca6c4d4b81efaeb49255b5973f3af.svg)\n\nIn general, a singular value decomposition \n\n$$\nA=U \\Sigma V^T\n$$\n\n \n\nof an $m \\times n$ matrix $A$ is not unique. However, we can always simplify it to the corresponding reduced singular value decomposition \n\n$$\nA=U_r \\Sigma_r V_r^T\n$$\n\n \n\nwith the following properties:\n\n- $\\Sigma_r$ is a square diagonal matrix of size $r\\times r,$ where $r\\leq \\min\\{m,n\\}$ is the rank of $A.$ The main diagonal of $\\Sigma_r$ contains the non-zero singular values $\\sigma_1, \\sigma_2, \\ldots, \\sigma_r$ of $A$ in descending order.\n\n- $U_r$ is a $m\\times r$ matrix obtained by keeping only the first $r$ columns of $U.$\n\n- $V_r$ is a $n \\times r$ matrix obtained by keeping only the first $r$ columns of $V.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163307 #edb/ref 1065 6075 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163307 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "924b4229-0b49-5d81-9255-b9be0ec07ab6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163307 #edb/ref 1067 "Reduced Singular Value Decomposition" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163309 #edb/ref 1064 "Recall that the least-squares solution of the system \n\n$$\nA\\mathbf x=\\mathbf{b},\n$$\n\nis a vector ${\\hat{\\mathbf{x}}}$ such that $\\| A{\\hat{\\mathbf{x}}} - \\mathbf{b} \\|$ is minimized. \n\nThe least-squares solution is not unique when $A$ has linearly dependent columns. However, it can be shown that a so-called **optimal least-squares solution** $\\mathbf{x^\\ast}$ always exists. The optimal least-squares solution is the solution to the least squares problem with the shortest possible length.\n\nIt turns out that the optimal least-squares solution can be found using the pseudoinverse of $A,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x^\\ast} = A^{+} \\mathbf{b}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163309 #edb/ref 1065 6078 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163309 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "6f731acb-e452-5433-88a5-1e2c66fd5a84" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163309 #edb/ref 1067 "The Optimal Least-Squares Solution" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163313 #edb/ref 1064 "There are many ways to find the pseudoinverse of a given matrix $A.$ One way is to use the singular value decomposition of $A{:}$\n\nSpecifically, if \n\n$$\nA=U \\Sigma V ^T\n$$\n\nis a singular value decomposition of $A,$ then the pseudoinverse of $A$ is given by\n\n$$\nA^+ = V \\, \\Sigma^+ \\, U^T,\n$$\n\n \nwhere $\\Sigma^+$ is the pseudoinverse of $\\Sigma.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163313 #edb/ref 1065 6102 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163313 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "bd110f79-cfe0-58f8-b7e6-ea9baa7814d9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163313 #edb/ref 1067 "Finding the Pseudoinverse Using Singular Value Decomposition" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163314 #edb/ref 1064 "For an *almost* zero ${\\color{red}{m}}\\times {\\color{blue}{n}}$ matrix with non-zero entries \n\n$$\n\\sigma_1, \\: \\sigma_2, \\: \\ldots, \\: \\sigma_r\n$$\n\non the main diagonal only, the corresponding pseudoinverse is the *almost* zero ${\\color{blue}{n}}\\times {\\color{red}{m}}$ matrix with non-zero entries \n\n$$\n{\\sigma_1^{-1}}, \\, {\\sigma_2^{-1}}, \\, \\ldots, \\, {\\sigma_r^{-1}}\n$$\n\non the main diagonal only.\n\nFor example, for the matrix $A,$ given by\n\n![](images/d6/d6d6a167747f99c5a48be59ae3e11ca7b5a9188e80ddeb63b8c0f193d991b18c.svg)\n\nwe calculate its pseudoinverse as follows:\n\n- **Step 1:** Replace the non-zero elements on the main diagonal with their multiplicative inverses.\n\n- **Step 2:** Transpose the resulting matrix.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{+} & ={[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0\\end{aligned}]}^{+} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{3}^{-1} & 0 \\\\ 0 & {4}^{-1} \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1/3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1/4 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163314 #edb/ref 1065 6103 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163314 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "8709222e-61fc-512e-82ff-3564c1513100" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163314 #edb/ref 1067 "The Pseudoinverse of an Almost Diagonal Matrix" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371783 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186371784 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371783 #edb/ref 1069 "q-138495" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371783 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371783 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\nU=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}],\\,Σ=[\\begin{aligned}2\\sqrt{10} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,V=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above. Given that $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ find the pseudoinverse ${A}^{+}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371783 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "730701f6-1ca8-5b40-956c-c0113bda87d0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371783 #edb/ref 1074 "If $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ then the pseudoinverse of $A$ is given by\n\n$$\nV\\,{Σ}^{+}\\,{U}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere ${Σ}^{+}$ is the pseudoinverse of $Σ.$\n\nFirst, notice that\n\n$$\nV=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}],\\,{Σ}^{+}=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{10}}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{U}^{T}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{+} & =V\\,{Σ}^{+}\\,{U}^{T} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{10}}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{20}[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{20}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{20}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371784 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186371785 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371784 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186371786 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371784 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186371787 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371784 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186371788 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371784 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186371789 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371784 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371784 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371784 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186371785 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371784 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "90c3d92d-95b1-5446-b383-5a0f0a13437f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371785 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{1}{20}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371785 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371785 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "0a66a064-024a-502e-9b04-347d649103a7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371786 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371786 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371786 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "9647bbdf-fcf2-5bad-a3b3-3e9694fc3084" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371787 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{1}{20}[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}]" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371787 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371787 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "2921dae6-65c8-54c1-bbee-1a5cf4f1be1b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371788 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{1}{10}[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371788 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371788 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "c1649e67-dae8-577c-9013-fb4d1dfa9a7a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371789 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{1}{10}[\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371789 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371789 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "f92721bb-f509-5c05-9e8a-6cddb7eeedbc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371797 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186371798 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371797 #edb/ref 1069 "q-138533" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371797 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371797 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\nU=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & 0 & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & 0 & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,Σ=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,V=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above. Given that $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ find the pseudoinverse ${A}^{+}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371797 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "203e330a-54ce-5347-9776-e74cc94e0d2d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371797 #edb/ref 1074 "If $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ then the pseudoinverse of $A$ is given by\n\n$$\nV\\,{Σ}^{+}\\,{U}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere ${Σ}^{+}$ is the pseudoinverse of $Σ.$\n\nFirst, notice that\n\n$$\nV=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{Σ}^{+}=\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{U}^{T}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & \\sqrt{2} \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{+} & =V\\,{Σ}^{+}\\,{U}^{T} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & \\sqrt{2} \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & \\sqrt{2} \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & \\sqrt{2} \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371798 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186371799 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371798 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186371800 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371798 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186371801 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371798 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/r